<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.4.2: Rewrite Squared Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02388</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.4.2: Rewrite Squared Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>dfce6d45-0123-4c84-83e2-f60cf738d1e2</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00001">Write each expression in standard form:</para>
          <para id="para-00002">\((4x+1)^2\)</para>
          <para id="para-00003">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00005">\(16x^2+8x+1\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00002">
        <problem id="prob-00002">
          <para id="para-00006">\((5x−2)^2\)</para>
          <para id="para-00007">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00002">
          <para id="para-00008">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00009">\(25x^2−20x+4\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00003">
        <problem id="prob-00003">
          <para id="para-00010">\((\frac{1}{2}x+7)^2\)</para>
          <para id="para-00011">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00003">
          <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00013">\(\frac{1}{4}x^2+7x+49\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00004">
        <problem id="prob-00004">
          <para id="para-00014">\((3x+n)^2\)</para>
          <para id="para-00015">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00004">
          <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00017">\(9x^2+2(3)(n)x+n^2\) or \(9x^2+6nx+n^2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00005">
        <problem id="prob-00005">
          <para id="para-00018">\((kx+m)^2\)</para>
          <para id="para-00019">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00005">
          <para id="para-00020">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00021">\(k^2x^2+2kmx+m^2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00006">
        <problem id="prob-00006">
          <para id="para-00022">Decide if each expression is a perfect square. If so, write an equivalent expression of the form \((kx+m)^2\). If not, suggest one change to turn it into a perfect square.</para>
          <para id="para-00023">\(4x^2+12x+9\)</para>
          <para id="para-00024">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00006">
          <para id="para-00025">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00026">Yes, a perfect square. \((2x+3)^2\)</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="exer-00007">
        <problem id="prob-00007">
          <para id="para-00027">\(4x^2+8x+25\)</para>
          <para id="para-00028">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00007">
          <para id="para-00029">Compare your answer:</para>
          <para id="para-00030">No, not a perfect square. Suggested changes include:</para>
          <list id="list-00001">
            <item>Changing the coefficient of the linear term to 20 gives \(4x^2+20x+25\), which is equivalent to \((2x+5)^2\).</item>
            <item>Changing the coefficient of the linear term to -20 gives \(4x^2−20x+25\), which is equivalent to \((2x−5)^2\).</item>
            <item>Changing the constant term to 4 gives \(4x^2+8x+4\), which is equivalent to \((2x+2)^2\).</item>
          </list>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <section id="sect-00002">
        <title>Video: Rewrite Squared Expressions</title>
        <para id="para-00031">Watch the following video to learn more about rewriting squared expressions.</para>
        <media alt="Placeholder for Alt Text">
          <iframe width="660" height="371.4" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/72e5797ce35541d183e0ff3955751a0ef621703e"/>
        </media>
      </section>
    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00001">
      <para id="para-00032">Find the equivalent factored expression for \(4x^2+12x+9\).</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Factoring Perfect Square Trinomials</title>
      <para id="para-00033">To factor <term class="glossary-tooltip" id="term-00001">perfect square trinomial</term>s, follow the formulas:</para>
      <para id="para-00034">\(a^2+2ab+b^2=(a+b)^2\)</para>
      <para id="para-00035">\(a^2-2ab+b^2=(a-b)^2\)</para>
      <para id="para-00036">Let's look at an example of how to factor perfect square trinomials.</para>
      <para id="para-00037"><emphasis>Example</emphasis></para>
      <para id="para-00038">Factor \(9x^2+12x+4\).</para>
      <para id="para-00039"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Does the trinomial fit the perfect square trinomial pattern, \(a^2+2ab+b^2\)? If so, write the first term as \(a^2\) and last constant as \(b^2\).</para>
      <list id="list-00002">
        <item>Is the first term a perfect square? Write it as a square, \(a^2\). Is \(9x^2\) a perfect square? Yes — write it as \((3x)^2\). \(\begin{array}{l}9x^2\;+\;12x\;+\;4 \\ (3x)^2\;\end{array}\)</item>
      </list>
      <list id="list-00003">
        <item>Is the last term a perfect square? Write it as a square, \(b^2\). Is 4 a perfect square? Yes — write it as \((2)^2\). \(\begin{array}{l}9x^2\;+\;12x\;+\;4 \\ (3x)^2\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;(2)^2\end{array}\)</item>
      </list>
      <list id="list-00004">
        <item>Check the middle term. Is it \(2ab\)? Is \(12x\) twice the product of \(3x\) and 2? Does it match? Yes, so we have a perfect trinomial.</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00040">\(\begin{array}{c}9x^2\;\;\;\;\;\;+\;\;\;\;\;\;12x\;\;\;\;\;\;+\;\;\;\;\;\;4\; \\ (3x)^2\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;(2)^2 \\ (3x)^2\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;(2)^2 \\ {\style{color:green}\searrow}\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;{\style{color:green}\swarrow} \\ 2(3x)(2) \\ 12x\;\end{array}\)</para>
      <para id="para-00041"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Factor the binomial into: \(a^2+2ab+b^2=(a+b)^2\). \(9x^2+12x+4\) \(\begin{array}{l}a^2\;\;\;\;\;\;+2\cdot a\cdot b+b^2 \\ \;({\style{color:red}3}{\style{color:red}x})^2\;+2 \cdot {\style{color:red}3}{\style{color:red}x} \cdot {\style{color:purple}2}+{\style{color:purple}2}^2\; \\ \;\;\;\;\;\;\;(a+b)^2\; \\ \;\;\;\;\;({\style{color:red}3}{\style{color:red}x}+{\style{color:green}2})^{\style{color:green}2}\end{array}\)</para>
      <para id="para-00042"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Check. \((3x+2)^2\) \((3x)^2+2 \cdot 3x \cdot 2+2^2\) \(9x^2+12x+4 ✔\)</para>
      <note class="try" id="note-00002">
        <title>Try It: Factoring Perfect Square Trinomials</title>
          <exercise id="exer-00008">
          <problem id="prob-00008">
            <para id="para-00043">Factor \(9x^2-30x+25\).</para>
            <para id="para-00044">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00008">
            <para id="para-00045">Here is how to factor a perfect square trinomial:</para>
            <para id="para-00046"><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - Write the first term as \(a^2\) and the last constant as \(b^2\). \(a^2=(3x)^2\), \(b^2=5^2\)</para>
            <para id="para-00047">Make sure the middle term is \(2ab\). \(2ab=2(3x)(5)=30x\)</para>
            <para id="para-00048"><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - Factor the binomial into: \(a^2-2ab+b^2=(a-b)^2\). \((3x-5)^2\)</para>
            <para id="para-00049"><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - Check.</para>
            <para id="para-00050">\((3x-5)(3x-5)\) \(=9x^2-15x-15x+25\) \(=9x^2-30x+25\)</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
  </content>
